Since $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$.

$\begin{aligned}$&\therefore $\lim _{x \rightarrow 2}$f(x)=f(2) \\
&\Rightarrow $\lim _{x \rightarrow 2}($x-1)^${\frac{1}{2-x}}$=k \quad $\left($1^{\infty} $\text{ form }\right)$\\
&\therefore e^{l=k}
\end{aligned}

where

$\begin{aligned}$&l=$\lim _{x \rightarrow 2}($x-1-1) $\times \frac{1}{2-x}=\lim _{x \rightarrow 2} \frac{x-2}{2-x}$\\
&$=\lim _{x \rightarrow 2} \left(\frac{x-2}{x-2}\right)$\\
&\Rightarrow k=e^{-1}
\end{aligned}

